.	http://www.emsl.pnl.gov/ecce:annotation		ECCE-QM, UPBE0/aug-cc-pVDZ, energy. This calculation shows the beryllium anion, the atom with one extra electron; the energy of the neutral atom minus the energy of this anion is the electron affinity. The 2s orbital of beryllium is full, so the extra electron has to go into the higher 2p orbital, and the anion is not more stable than the atom.
